# contrib/action.yml
# Composite action used BY A DIVISION'S OWN REPOSITORY to request a perimeter change.
#
# The division's repository never applies anything and needs NO permission in Access Context Manager —
# and, since the contract moved to a release asset, NO identity in Google Cloud at all. It needs exactly
# one thing: a GitHub App token on THIS repository, with `Contents: Read-only` + `Actions: Read and write`.
# See the `app-token` input for why those two and not the pair intuition suggests.
#
# WHY workflow_dispatch AND NOT repository_dispatch — the reason is a permission, not a preference.
# `POST /repos/{o}/{r}/dispatches` requires Contents WRITE, i.e. the right to write CODE in the perimeter
# repository (measured; the table is in contrib/README.md). Compose that with a default branch that has no
# branch protection — a paid feature for private repositories on several GitHub plans — and with apply
# running automatically from that branch, and a division's credential becomes a path to mutating the
# organization's boundary while bypassing every gate: schema, OPA, budget and pre-flight all run on
# `pull_request`, and a push straight to the default branch runs none of them.
# `POST /repos/{o}/{r}/actions/workflows/{file}/dispatches` requires `actions: write`, which can START a
# workflow and cannot write a single byte of code. The narrative — "one right: to cause a pull request to
# be opened" — becomes true of the token itself, not just of the gates behind it.
#
# WHY the contract is fetched from a release and not from `gs://…`: reading the bucket copy requires the
# division's repository to federate an identity into GCP and hold a grant on the contract prefix. Thirty
# divisions meant thirty grants — issued so that someone could read 4 KB of JSON. The bucket copy still
# exists for machine consumers outside GitHub; it is simply no longer on this path. Both copies are written
# by the SAME apply step (see apply.yml), so there is no "which one is current" question to ask.
#
# WHY not let them apply: Access Context Manager rewrites the whole org-level policy on every change and
# guards it with an eTag. Parallel appliers do NOT lose rules silently — the loser fails loudly with
# `Error 400: The eTag provided ... does not match` (measured: ~80-100% of time-overlapping runs). That is not
# reassuring: with two mutators every other merge randomly fails and needs a retry, and each new division
# raises the collision rate. On top of that, `servicePerimeters.update` cannot be scoped below the
# organization, so every such repository would get the right to change the boundary for the entire company.
#
# WHY the contract + gates bundle instead of a submodule: a submodule hands over the WHOLE perimeter
# repository — including every division's service accounts (perimeter/members/) and your corporate IP ranges
# (perimeter/access-levels/). Validating one file of your own needs neither.
#
# Usage in the division's repository:
#
#   - uses: actions/checkout@11d5960a326750d5838078e36cf38b85af677262 # v4.4.0
#   - uses: ORG/gcp-vpc-sc/contrib@main
#     with:
#       member-file: vpc-sc/prj-example-vertex-prod.yaml
#       perimeter-repo: ORG/gcp-vpc-sc
#       app-token: ${{ steps.app.outputs.token }}

name: Request VPC-SC perimeter change
description: Validate a member declaration against the published contract, then ask the perimeter repository to open a PR.

inputs:
  member-file:
    description: Path to the member YAML in THIS repository.
    required: true
  perimeter-repo:
    description: ORG/REPO of the perimeter repository.
    required: true
  app-token:
    description: >
      Token of a GitHub App installed on both repositories, with exactly two permissions on the perimeter
      repository: `Contents: Read-only` and `Actions: Read and write`. MEASURED, because every part of that
      answer is counter-intuitive. `Contents: Read-only` is what downloads the gates bundle and the contract
      (both are release assets, and release assets sit under Contents). `Actions: Read and write` is what the
      last step needs: `POST /repos/{o}/{r}/actions/workflows/{file}/dispatches` returns 204 with
      `actions: write` and 403 with `contents: write` alone — the two permissions are disjoint in BOTH
      directions, so a token that can start this workflow cannot write a single file in the perimeter
      repository. `pull_requests` is not needed at all: the pull request is opened on the other side by
      `external-intake.yml`, with the perimeter repository's own GITHUB_TOKEN. What the token can still do
      with `actions: write` and we do not hide: re-run and cancel workflows, and delete run logs. Narrower
      than code write, not zero. GITHUB_TOKEN will NOT work here in any case — it is scoped to the
      repository it was issued for.
    required: true
  intake-workflow:
    description: >
      File name of the intake workflow in the perimeter repository. A file name, not a path: the API
      addresses workflows by base name or numeric id. It has to exist on the perimeter repository's DEFAULT
      branch — GitHub only accepts `workflow_dispatch` for workflows present there.
    required: false
    default: external-intake.yml
  gates-version:
    description: >
      Release tag of the gates bundle, or `latest`. Pin it if you want reproducible validation; use `latest`
      to see new rules the moment the platform ships them.
    required: false
    default: latest

runs:
  using: composite
  steps:
    - name: install tooling
      shell: bash
      run: |
        pip install --quiet pyyaml check-jsonschema
        curl -sSfL -o /tmp/conftest.tar.gz \
          https://github.com/open-policy-agent/conftest/releases/download/v0.56.0/conftest_0.56.0_Linux_x86_64.tar.gz
        tar -xzf /tmp/conftest.tar.gz -C /tmp conftest
        sudo mv /tmp/conftest /usr/local/bin/

    # Gates = RULES only (schemas + rego). No member data, no IP ranges — see publish-gates.yml.
    - name: fetch the gates bundle
      shell: bash
      env:
        GH_TOKEN: ${{ inputs.app-token }}
      run: |
        if [ "${{ inputs.gates-version }}" = "latest" ]; then
          gh release download --repo "${{ inputs.perimeter-repo }}" --pattern 'gates.tar.gz' --clobber
        else
          gh release download "${{ inputs.gates-version }}" --repo "${{ inputs.perimeter-repo }}" \
            --pattern 'gates.tar.gz' --clobber
        fi
        tar -xzf gates.tar.gz

    # Contract = the list of options you may choose from (profiles, access level names, your own projects).
    #
    # The `contract` release tag is deliberately NOT an input and deliberately NOT pinnable. Gates are RULES,
    # so pinning them buys reproducible validation; the contract is a snapshot of STATE, so a pinned copy
    # means validating against profiles and access levels that may no longer exist — a green run that lies.
    # The perimeter repository moves this tag on every apply, exactly like `Cache-Control: no-store` on the
    # bucket copy.
    - name: fetch the contract
      shell: bash
      env:
        GH_TOKEN: ${{ inputs.app-token }}
      run: |
        gh release download contract --repo "${{ inputs.perimeter-repo }}" \
          --pattern 'contract.json' --clobber
        echo "contract from $(python3 -c 'import json;print(json.load(open("contract.json"))["schema_version"])') schema, \
        $(python3 -c 'import json;print(len(json.load(open("contract.json"))["profiles"]))') profiles available"

    # Validating HERE is the point: the team sees the error in its own pipeline, in seconds, instead of in
    # a rejected PR in someone else's repository.
    - name: validate the declaration
      shell: bash
      run: |
        bash ./gates/validate-local.sh \
          --member "${{ inputs.member-file }}" \
          --gates ./gates \
          --contract ./contract.json

    # change_ref is built by the ACTION, not read from the file — a self-declared reference proves nothing,
    # and the perimeter repository cross-checks it against the repository that actually sent the dispatch.
    #
    # `workflow_dispatch` carries INPUTS, and inputs are flat strings: at most 10 of them, 65535 characters
    # each. The declaration is nested (profiles carry parameters), so it travels as ONE input holding JSON
    # rather than exploded into fields. Two reasons, both about the failure mode rather than tidiness:
    # a flattened payload would need a re-assembly step on the other side, and every profile parameter added
    # to the catalogue would silently exceed 10 inputs — an error that only appears for the division whose
    # request happens to be the eleventh field. `project_id` travels a SECOND time as its own input because
    # `concurrency` is evaluated before any step runs and cannot parse JSON; the other side refuses the
    # request if the two disagree, so the duplicate cannot become a way to serialise under a false name.
    #
    # `ref` is read from the API rather than hardcoded to `main`: the endpoint runs the workflow FILE as it
    # exists at that ref, so pointing it anywhere else would run a version of the intake that no one
    # reviewed. The other side refuses to run off its default branch anyway — that check and this one are
    # deliberately on both ends, because either alone is a single point of "and then somebody changed it".
    - name: request the change
      shell: bash
      env:
        GH_TOKEN: ${{ inputs.app-token }}
        MEMBER_FILE: ${{ inputs.member-file }}
        PERIMETER_REPO: ${{ inputs.perimeter-repo }}
        INTAKE_WORKFLOW: ${{ inputs.intake-workflow }}
        PR_NUMBER: ${{ github.event.pull_request.number || github.run_number }}
      run: |
        set -euo pipefail
        CHANGE_REF="pr:${GITHUB_REPOSITORY}#${PR_NUMBER}"
        REF="$(gh api "repos/${PERIMETER_REPO}" --jq .default_branch)"
        export CHANGE_REF REF
        python3 - > dispatch-inputs.json <<'PY'
        import json, os, yaml
        m = yaml.safe_load(open(os.environ["MEMBER_FILE"]))
        m["change_ref"] = os.environ["CHANGE_REF"]
        print(json.dumps({"ref": os.environ["REF"], "inputs": {
            "member": json.dumps(m, sort_keys=True),
            "project_id": str(m["project_id"]),
            "source_repository": os.environ["GITHUB_REPOSITORY"],
            "source_sha": os.environ["GITHUB_SHA"],
            "change_ref": os.environ["CHANGE_REF"]}}))
        PY
        gh api --method POST \
          "repos/${PERIMETER_REPO}/actions/workflows/${INTAKE_WORKFLOW}/dispatches" \
          --input dispatch-inputs.json
        rm -f dispatch-inputs.json
        echo "Zgłoszenie wysłane jako ${CHANGE_REF} (${INTAKE_WORKFLOW}@${REF}) — PR powstanie w ${PERIMETER_REPO}."
